    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i checked the temp and it seems consistent around 390F. the pressure is high too. the weird thing is the ink seems to just... vanish? like the design looks okay on the paper but when it hits the fabric it loses all the saturation and detail. it's almost like the color isnt staying put. any idea why the pigment would fail to bond like that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The ink is likely vaporizing before it can bond because the temperature on the hot surface is actually lower than the iron reads, especially if you have a thick packaging pad between the iron and the platen. You should use an infrared thermometer to measure the actual temperature of the paper edge and the platen, then increase these by 5-10 degrees above the setpoint. Additionally, ensure you are using sublimation transfer paper that is specifically calibrated for polyester rather than cotton, as standard paper transfers designed for cotton often lack the pigment density needed to bond properly to synthetic fibers.",
